# The suites of tools/suite.list on Windows (Git Bash of the GitHub runner, Maat 1.6.0): one CI job per group
# (.github/workflows/ci.yml, job windows-gruppo), `tools/suite.sh --gruppi tools/suite-gruppi-windows.list --gruppo k/n`.
# Lines: «<group> [VAR=value …] <entry>» (the entry runs in that group, with those variables) and «- <entry> <reason>»
# (left out on Windows: counted as covered, written as ESCLUSA in riepilogo.txt). Every entry of suite.list in exactly
# one line; groups 1..n (tools/suite.sh refuses otherwise, tools/suite.test.sh checks the link with ci.yml).
# 1.8.0 (CI): seven groups instead of five, balanced on the seconds of the first CI of 1.8.0 on the Windows runner
# (the longer of its two runs): group 1 ≈ 1424 s, 2 ≈ 1093, 3 ≈ 1050, 4 ≈ 970, 5 ≈ 955, 6 ≈ 941, 7 ≈ 647. The three
# longest suites (driver, run-phase, the expected outputs) are alone in their groups. The runner is about three times
# faster than the local Windows machine (gates 234 s against 826, 1.6.0).
# 1.8.0 (CI of the merged tree): the driver grew past the per-suite limit (TIMEOUT driver dopo 1800 s); its 1.8.0
# sections went to driver-esiti (driver.test.sh 207 cases + driver-esiti 126 = the 333 of before; on Linux 272 s + 49 s).
# The driver keeps its group (its sections 1-13 are the 1424 s measured before B8-B13); driver-esiti goes to group 7.
# Locally the same set: bash tools/suite.sh --gruppi tools/suite-gruppi-windows.list <results dir> (from Git Bash).

# left out on Windows
- test-fast            needs rsync and flock: on Windows it runs in WSL, which the runner does not have (locally at release)
- counterproof         needs rsync and flock: on Windows it runs in WSL, which the runner does not have (locally at release)
- vitest-classify      needs rsync and flock: on Windows it runs in WSL, which the runner does not have (locally at release)
- comandi-equivalenza  needs rsync and flock: on Windows it runs in WSL, which the runner does not have (locally at release)
- tools/oracolo.test.sh  about 70 minutes in Git Bash on the local machine; logic that does not depend on the platform: it runs on Linux and macOS

# group 1: the driver (1424 s)
1 driver

# group 2: run-phase (1093 s; 1684 s on the CI of 1.8.0 main; over 1800 s with 1.8.1: the Windows limits rose, ci.yml)
2 run-phase

# group 3: the expected outputs of the public corpus (1050 s)
3 tools/esempi-equiv.test.sh

# group 4: cycle-state (888 s) and short ones
4 cycle-state
4 tools/suite.test.sh
4 stop-hook
4 session-start
4 phase-usage
4 nomi_test
4 transcript
4 blocks-independent

# group 5: plan-check (644 s), gates (243 s) and short ones
5 plan-check
5 gates
5 context-pack
5 harness-merge
5 maat-config

# group 6: finalize, messages, ciclo-worktree, cycle-report, phase-events
6 finalize
6 messages
6 ciclo-worktree
6 cycle-report
6 phase-events

# group 7: the 1.8.0 sections of the driver (driver-esiti), maat-doctor and the short ones
7 driver-esiti
7 maat-doctor
7 bash-node
7 guard
7 test-review-surface
7 genericita
7 senza-wsl
7 spazi
7 maat-paths
